### Phase 2: Pattern Recognition & Array/String Mastery (Tasks 13–35)
**Duration:** 2 weeks

This phase focuses on recognizing problem patterns and mastering string/array manipulation—skills that appear in 40% of senior interviews.

**2A: Two-Pointer & Sliding Window (Tasks 13–18)**
- Merge sorted arrays with O(1) extra space
- Container with most water (optimization from brute force)
- Longest substring without repeating characters (sliding window with hash map)
- Trapping rain water (two-pointer approach)
- Remove duplicates in-place (interview-style constraints)
- Minimum window substring (KMP + sliding window hybrid)

**2B: Dynamic Programming on Arrays (Tasks 19–27)**
- House robber variations (1D DP with state compression)
- Maximum subarray (Kadane's algorithm and variations)
- Coin change problem (bottom-up DP vs. BFS)
- Edit distance (matrix DP with space optimization)
- Jump game variants (greedy vs. DP analysis)
- Longest increasing subsequence (binary search + DP)
- Partition equal subset sum (knapsack variant)
- Max product subarray (state tracking for negative numbers)
- Distinct subsequences (combinatorial DP)

**2C: String & Pattern Matching (Tasks 28–35)**
- Implement KMP algorithm from scratch
- Longest palindromic substring (expand-around-center vs. DP)
- Word break problem (DP vs. trie-based solutions)
- Implement regex matcher (backtracking with memoization)
- Anagram detection at scale (multiple approaches)
- Palindrome partitioning (backtracking with DP pruning)
- Integer to words conversion (state machine approach)
- Permutation in string (sliding window + frequency map)

```javascript
// Example: Longest Palindromic Substring with DP
function longestPalindrome(s) {
  const n = s.length;
  if (n < 2) return s;

  // dp[i][j] = true if s[i..j] is palindrome
  const dp = Array(n).fill(null).map(() => Array(n).fill(false));
  let start = 0, maxLen = 1;

  // Every single character is a palindrome
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    dp[i][i] = true;
  }

  // Check for length 2
  for (let i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
    if (s[i] === s[i + 1]) {
      dp[i][i + 1] = true;
      start = i;
      maxLen = 2;
    }
  }

  // Check for length 3 and more
  for (let len = 3; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let i = 0; i < n - len + 1; i++) {
      const j = i + len - 1;
      if (s[i] === s[j] && dp[i + 1][j - 1]) {
        dp[i][j] = true;
        start = i;
        maxLen = len;
      }
    }
  }

  return s.substring(start, start + maxLen);
}

// Time: O(n²), Space: O(n²) - interview-friendly with clear state representation
```

